WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.466 --> 00:00:03.300
[music]

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.533
[Instructor] Now the Player 
can pick up this Powerup,

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:11.700
and then it'll open up the Powerup
Indicator that will show

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.066
when we have our Powerup
so we can blast our opponents

5
00:00:14.066 --> 00:00:16.066
away off of the environment.

6
00:00:17.666 --> 00:00:20.400
It's still a little bit too easy,
so we're going to introduce

7
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.600
a bit more challenge into the game
by spawning multiple enemies

8
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.666
all over the screen over time.

9
00:00:25.966 --> 00:00:29.166
In our SpawnManager class,
we have exactly everything

10
00:00:29.166 --> 00:00:32.300
that we need to be able to spawn
multiple enemies over time.

11
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.766
And in order to do that, we're going
to use something called a "for() loop."

12
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.333
Now, a "for() loop"

13
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.533
is similar
to an "if() statement,"

14
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.500
where with an if() statement,
we used a condition

15
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.500
to be able to see if something was true.

16
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.500
For example, with something in our game,
if the Player picks up a Powerup.

17
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.200
A for() loop will enable
us to control the iterations

18
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.333
that something goes through.
So if we want something

19
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:56.833
to execute 5 times, we can do that.

20
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:00.266
For example, if we want 
to have our Instantiate() method

21
00:01:00.266 --> 00:01:02.666
happen 5 times,
we can use a for() loop.

22
00:01:02.666 --> 00:01:04.600
So, in our Start() method,

23
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.900
if we type in the keyword "for,"

24
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.566
the way a for() loop works
is that we actually need

25
00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:11.866
3 different Parameters

26
00:01:11.866 --> 00:01:13.866
to be able to use this loop.

27
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:17.133
First, we need to tell
the for() loop where to start.

28
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.166
So we're going to create
a variable in here

29
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.733
for our start point, and we'll just
call it "i,"

30
00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:24.900
and we'll set it to 0.

31
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.466
Now we need to tell the for() loop
the condition of when it should stop.

32
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:32.100
As long as "i < 3,"

33
00:01:32.866 --> 00:01:37.000
then it should continue.
Once i is not less than 3,

34
00:01:37.366 --> 00:01:39.400
this will make our for() loop
stop running,

35
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:42.233
since the condition
won't be true anymore.

36
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.366
And then we need
to tell the for() loop

37
00:01:45.466 --> 00:01:49.066
how we're going get
i from 0 to 3.

38
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:52.566
So we can say "i

39
00:01:52.566 --> 00:01:54.700
= i

40
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.800
+ 1."

41
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.300
Another way that we can
do this is similar

42
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.933
to how we multiply values by itself.

43
00:02:01.933 --> 00:02:04.700
We can just say "i += 1."

44
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.766
But if we actually want
an even shorter way

45
00:02:07.766 --> 00:02:09.966
of saying i += 1

46
00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:15.133
we can actually just say "i++"

47
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.533
and C# will know that this
should just

48
00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:19.366
add 1 to i

49
00:02:19.466 --> 00:02:21.833
every time the for() loop goes through.

50
00:02:22.066 --> 00:02:24.866
So what'll happen is
i will be set to 0,

51
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.200
it'll make an enemy,
and it'll add 1 to i,

52
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.200
so that i is equal to 1.

53
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:35.500
Then it'll make another enemy,
same thing, i becomes 2.

54
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:38.600
And then it'll make 
another enemy,

55
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.633
adds 1 to i.

56
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:42.433
And then it'll stop making enemies.

57
00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:44.633
And then we just need to wrap
this entire thing

58
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.833
in its own block,

59
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.666
using our curly braces.
So now this'll create 3 enemies.

60
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:54.300
Now when we press Play,

61
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.866
you should see
that we have 3 enemies spawn,

62
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.466
because they were all
created using our for() loop.

63
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.100
Now just to help us a little bit,
and clean up our code,

64
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.266
we're going to create 
a new method

65
00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:10.333
down at the bottom. 
So we'll call this "void...

66
00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:14.600
SpawnEnemyWave()"

67
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.966
so that this way
we can spawn our enemies.

68
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.933
And then we can take our code here,

69
00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:25.833
put it in SpawnEnemyWave(),

70
00:03:26.966 --> 00:03:28.966
and then just call "SpawnEnemyWave();."

71
00:03:32.366 --> 00:03:36.166
So, what you're going to do,
is you're going to create a new method

72
00:03:36.166 --> 00:03:37.966
called void SpawnEnemyWave().

73
00:03:39.966 --> 00:03:42.366
In your method, 
you're going to use a for() loop.

74
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.033
You're going to use the variable i,
set it to 0.

75
00:03:47.466 --> 00:03:50.633
When i < 3,
then this for() loop will stop.

76
00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:53.500
Increment i + 1 every time,

77
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.000
and then just call
the Instantiate() method

78
00:03:57.766 --> 00:03:59.800
for our enemyPrefab.

79
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.400
And then call SpawnEnemyWave()
in your Start() method.

80
00:04:04.433 --> 00:04:06.433
Now it's your turn.

81
00:04:07.066 --> 00:04:09.433
[music]
